+# Deterministic simulation: the network as data
+
+A cluster's failures are the interesting part of it, and the hardest part to test: a partition, a
+crash, a dropped message — each one is a thing that happens *to* the system at a moment nobody chose.
+This page is the project's answer, and it is the same answer every serious distributed system has
+found: **make the failure an input**.
+
+## The playbook
+
+A scenario is a text file. One step per line; `#` starts a comment.
+
+```text
+partition n2 n3
+write a 1
+write b 2
+heal n2 n3
+write c 3
+write d 4
+```
+
+Five faults:
+
+| fault | meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `partition <a> <b>` | nothing gets between `a` and `b`, in either direction |
+| `heal <a> <b>` | the link works again |
+| `drop <n>` | the next `n` messages are lost, in delivery order |
+| `crash <a>` | `a` stops answering |
+| `restart <a>` | `a` answers again |
+
+## The two properties
+
+1. **The same seed is the same run.** A seed decides the order events happen in — which node the
+   client talks to, which messages arrive first — so `(seed, playbook)` produces one transcript,
+   byte for byte. That is what makes a failure reproducible: a bug found by a seed is a bug somebody
+   else can run.
+2. **Another seed is another run.** A schedule that never varies tests one interleaving. The
+   scenarios the course ships are run under several seeds, and the invariants have to hold under
+   all of them.
+
+## A playbook that says nothing is refused
+
+`partition n1 n1` (a node isolated from itself), `heal` on a link that is not partitioned, `drop 0`,
+two `crash`es of one node with no restart between: each is a step that cannot change anything, and a
+scenario built from steps that cannot change anything is a scenario that proves nothing. The check
+runs before the scenario does.
+
+## Why this is possible at all
+
+Because the cluster's rules are a value. `5.3` put the Raft state machine in a type with no sockets
+and no clock; `5.10` made the state machine a pure function; everything since has kept that shape.
+A playbook can only drive something that takes messages and returns messages — a system that reached
+for the network inside its rules could not be driven by a playbook, and its failures could only be
+tested by waiting for them.
+
+## Where it goes next
+
+`5.14` reads a partition out of a playbook and asserts what the minority did; `5.16` turns the
+faults into a matrix with an expected behaviour per cell; `5.17` puts a budget on the latency the
+schedule produces. The vocabulary stays small on purpose: a fault vocabulary nobody can exhaust is a
+vocabulary that hides bugs.
